    29	## The contract
    30	
    31	1. **Journal mode, side files, opening.**
    32	   - The live store is in WAL mode, set by the writer when it creates the store. Production's stores stay as they are.
    33	   - The `-wal` and `-shm` files sit beside the store on a local disk. **No reader reaches a live store through a container bind mount
    34	     or a network share.**
    35	   - **Opening precondition:** every reader runs as the writer's user, and the store's directory is writable by it. SQLite needs the
    36	     `-wal` and `-shm` files readable, or permission to create them, to open a WAL store with `mode=ro`.
    37	   - Readers open with `mode=ro`, never `immutable=1`.
    38	   - A reader that cannot open the store, or cannot begin a read transaction, fails loudly with the store's path. It never serves an
    39	     empty or stale answer in its place.
    40	   - Readers set a busy timeout and retry `SQLITE_BUSY` within it. SQLite documents rare WAL cases where a read returns busy, for
    41	     example while the WAL index is being rebuilt.
    42	   - The guarantee is that readers do not block the writer's commits, not that the writer never waits.
    43	2. **One committed state per request, one connection per concurrent request.**
    44	   - Each API request and each MCP tool call runs every read inside one explicit read transaction: `BEGIN`, the reads, the end.
    45	   - The transaction always ends, failure included (`finally`).
    46	   - **MCP opens a connection per tool call,** as the API does per request, in place of today's one process-wide `LEDGER`.
    47	     Overlapping calls under streamable HTTP each hold their own transaction and never share one.
    48	   - The service's start-up reads run the same way. MCP's object list is computed per call.
    49	3. **Every date bound, resolved in one function.**
    50	   - Today 13 places resolve a bound as `x or default_as_of(conn)`: `service.py` ×9 (`summaries`, `views`, `view`, `evidence`,
    51	     `history`'s `to_day`, `record`, `changes`' `to_day`, `evaluate`, …), `bundle.py` ×2, `idea.py` ×1 and `search.py` ×1. An
    52	     explicit date bypasses any check placed in `default_as_of`.
    53	   - **The owner:** one function, `service.resolve_bound(conn, bound)`, receives every upper bound, explicit or omitted, in its
    54	     caller's read transaction.
    55	     - An omitted bound gives the last completed day.
    56	     - An explicit bound later than the last completed day is refused, naming that day.
    57	     - An explicit bound on or before it passes.
    58	   - Every one of the 13 sites calls it, and lower bounds (`from_day`, `since_day`) are unaffected.
    59	   - **A structural check** fails the suite when any module calls `default_as_of` outside `resolve_bound`, or resolves a bound by
    60	     `or`. A behaviour test per caller gives each an explicit future bound and an omitted one.
    61	4. **The first day** (open defect 10).
    62	   - Before any day completes, every request and tool call is refused, including the streamed Idea route before any header.
    63	   - An empty store (no step) and a legacy ledger read as today.
    64	4a. **Search: visibility before ranking and limits, and a published index day.**
    65	   - **Lexical:**
    66	     - today `store.search_views` ranks the whole `views_fts` table by SQLite's `rank`, whose statistics cover the whole index;
    67	     - so filtering before the limit is not enough, because unfinished views would still move the ranks of visible ones;
    68	     - **the lexical rank is computed over the visible corpus only:** per request, the visible candidates' texts (from
    69	       `service.summaries` at the request's bound; tens to hundreds of views) are put into an in-memory full-text table with the same
    70	       trigram tokenizer, and ranked there;
    71	     - its statistics are then those of the completed-day library, equal to what the same query gives on that day's backup.
    72	   - **The store's identity:** a random identifier the writer stores in the store when it creates it (a one-row table). A store
    73	     replaced at the same path, even with the same first sequence and day, gets a new one.
    74	   - **The index publishes a day:**
    75	     - it holds vectors only for views visible as of its published day, and their text as of that day;
    76	     - a refresh embeds into a staging table, each staged row keyed by **(store identity, view, the text's sha256, the embedding
    77	       model)**. A vector depends only on its text and model, so the target day is not part of the key;
    78	     - only when every view visible at the target day has a row under the current store identity does one index transaction move
    79	       those rows in and advance the published day;
    80	     - a failure leaves the published day and its vectors unchanged;
    81	     - **one reuse rule:** any later refresh, for the same or a newer target day, reuses a staged row exactly when all four key parts
    82	       match. Every row under another store identity is discarded.
    83	     - **Published vectors are tied to the store too:** the index records the store identity it was published for. Search uses a
    84	       published vector only when:
    85	       - that identity equals the store's;
    86	       - the published day is on or before the store's last completed day;
    87	       - the vector's text sha equals the view's text at the request's bound.
    88	     - Otherwise the view counts as unindexed: search runs lexically, says so, and the replacement's index is published by a
    89	       refresh.
    90	     - A store restored from a snapshot keeps its identity, but a published day after its last completed day, or a changed text,
    91	       still disqualifies the vectors.
    92	     - A view written during an unfinished day is never embedded and never sent to the embedding service.
    93	   - **A request's state:**
    94	     - search reads the index's published day and uses vectors only for views visible at the smaller of that day and the request's
    95	       bound;
    96	     - if a visible view has no published vector, search runs lexically and says so, as today;
    97	     - the ledger and the index are separate files, so the published day is what ties them.
    98	   - On a first-day store nothing is indexed, and search is refused as in row 4.
    99	5. **Checkpoints and disk: what is guaranteed, and the disk-exhaustion behaviour.**
   100	   - The writer sets `journal_size_limit` (proposed 64 MB) and keeps SQLite's automatic passive checkpoint.
   101	   - After each completed day it asks for a truncating checkpoint with a short busy timeout.
   102	   - **Guaranteed:**
   103	     - readers do not block the writer's commits;
   104	     - after a completed day whose truncating checkpoint succeeds, the `-wal` is within the limit;
   105	     - if the truncating checkpoint fails on 3 consecutive completed days, the writer stops before the next day with a recorded
   106	       reason.
   107	   - **Not guaranteed:** a byte bound on the `-wal` within an unfinished day, or across the failed days. Its growth there is the
   108	     day's own writes, which the contract does not bound.
   109	   - **Disk:**
   110	     - before each day the writer checks the free space on the store's disk against a minimum stated in the run's limits file
   111	       (proposed: 20 times the store's size), and stops before the day with a recorded reason if it is below;
   112	     - a write that still meets a full filesystem mid-day (extending the store, its `-wal` or a side file) fails, and the writer
   113	       exits non-zero as in a crash (row 6);
   114	     - its stop reason may then be lost too, if the run's log is on the full disk;
   115	     - **the guarantee in that case is the readers' boundedness** (they keep the last completed day), not the record.
   116	       The process's exit status, seen by its supervisor, is the signal.
   117	6. **A crash.**
   118	   - WAL drops the unfinished transaction. The day's **already committed writes stay** in the store.
   119	   - Readers stay bounded to the last completed day. Every row written during the unfinished day is dated by that day's processing
   120	     steps, after the last completed day, so it is outside every allowed bound.
   121	   - **The writer does not resume** (today's loop). The store is served as of its last completed day until it is replaced.
   122	   - Resuming a stopped live store is a separate design, not covered here.
   123	7. **Who is writing: a signal outside the store, and copies.**
   124	   - **The writer's lock, named by the file, not the path:**
   125	     - the lock file sits in one fixed lock folder (`~/.hivemind-locks/`), named by the store file's device and inode numbers
   126	       (`<device>-<inode>.writer.lock`);
   127	     - both are read with `stat`, which reads no content of the store;
   128	     - a symlink resolves to the same file, and a hard link shares its inode, so every alias of a store reaches the same lock.
   129	   - **The writer locks before it writes:** it creates the store file empty, takes the lock without waiting, and only then opens it
   130	     as a database. If the lock is held (by preflight), it refuses to start, with that reason.
   131	   - For its whole life the writer holds the lock (`flock`); the operating system releases it on any exit, crash included.
   132	   - **Preflight, before it touches a store, always takes the same lock without waiting,** creating the lock file if it is absent.
   133	     There is no "no lock file" branch, so a writer cannot start between a check and a copy:
   134	     - **if the lock is held, a writer is running:** preflight refuses, naming the run's snapshot script (AGENTS §9's sanctioned way);
   135	     - otherwise preflight keeps the lock through its copy (the backup API, from a `mode=ro` source) and releases it after;
   136	     - historical stores and production's are copied the same way; taking a fresh lock on them is harmless.
   137	     - AGENTS §9 remains the only protection for production's own writer, which takes no such lock; changing it is out of scope.
   138	   - **Snapshots:** the snapshot script is the one toucher that copies a store while its writer runs, with the backup API.
   139	     Preflight accepts its snapshots like any store with no writer.
   140	   - **Orderly stop:**
   141	     - at its last day the writer tries to leave the store in rollback-journal mode (`journal_mode=delete`, with a short busy
   142	       timeout);
   143	     - if service readers hold it, the store stays in WAL mode and the writer records "stopped in order, WAL kept" in its run's
   144	       outcome;
   145	     - either way it then releases its lock.
   146	     - Journal mode is never used as the sign of a live store; the lock is.
   147	8. **AGENTS §9 ("Never open a live run's databases") stays in force.**
   148	   - **Proposed decision P1, to be resolved before any live-service operation and not assumed by any proof:** the product's API and
   149	     MCP server may read a live WAL store under rows 1 to 7. Every other toucher above uses a backup-API snapshot.
   150	   - The proofs run on test stores made for them, never on a live run.
