   152	## State × event (the store's state at the request's transaction × the request)
   153	
   154	| State | Bounds omitted | Upper bound ≤ last completed day | Upper bound > last completed day | Start-up reads | Copy |
   155	|---|---|---|---|---|---|
   156	| Empty store | as today | as today | as today | as today | consistent |
   157	| First day in progress | refused (4) | — | refused (4) | wait; requests refused | consistent (an in-progress copy, read under the same rules) |
   158	| A later day in progress | the last completed day | that bound | refused (3) | the last completed day | consistent |
   159	| A day completing during a request | the request's own transaction | same | same | same | consistent |
   160	| Crashed mid-day, not resumed | the last completed day | that bound | refused (3) | the last completed day | consistent |
   161	| `-wal`/`-shm` absent and directory not writable | fails loudly (1) | same | same | fails loudly | — |
   162	
   163	## Proof plan ($0, on test stores made for it; nothing built until the council accepts the contract)
   164	
   165	1. **WAL mode:** the writer's store is in WAL mode with the size limit set; production's stores are unchanged.
   166	2. **Every upper bound:**
   167	   - the structural check that no bound is resolved outside `resolve_bound`;
   168	   - for each of the 13 callers, an explicit future bound is refused naming the last completed day, an explicit past bound passes,
   169	     and an omitted bound gives the last completed day;
   170	   - lower bounds are unaffected.
   171	3. **One state per request:** an API request held between its first and last read while the writer commits a judgement and
   172	   completes a day returns the state at its start, and the writer's commits succeed. Run on a first-day store and a store with one
   173	   completed day.
   174	4. **Start-up and MCP concurrency:**
   175	   - two overlapping MCP calls across a writer's commit each return their own transaction's state;
   176	   - a call that fails mid-read leaves no open transaction and no open connection;
   177	   - a service started on a first-day store refuses requests and builds its index after the first day completes;
   178	   - MCP's object list after a later day includes that day's objects;
   179	   - no transaction is open between MCP calls.
   180	5. **Checkpoint and disk:**
   181	   - below the free-space minimum, the writer stops before the day with its reason, and readers serve the last completed day;
   182	   - a database-full error mid-day (a test store with `max_page_count`) stops the writer as a crash, and readers are unaffected;
   183	   - a filesystem-full error mid-day: the store on a small disk image (`hdiutil`, a few MB), filled while the `-wal` grows. The
   184	     writer exits non-zero, and readers serve the last completed day. Whether its reason was recorded is reported, not required;
   185	   - with a long read held, the writer commits;
   186	   - after the read ends, a checkpoint completes, and a reset brings the `-wal` within the limit;
   187	   - with a reader held across three completed days, the writer stops before the fourth with its reason recorded, and readers serve
   188	     the last completed day.
   189	6. **Opening precondition:** with `-wal`/`-shm` absent and the directory read-only, a reader fails loudly. A busy read is retried
   190	   within the timeout.
   191	7. **A crash:** the writer is killed after committed writes mid-day.
   192	   - Every route's answer, at every allowed bound, equals the answer from a backup taken at the last completed day.
   193	   - The search index and the text sent to the embedding service (recorded by a fake transport) equal the backup's.
   194	   - The partial day's rows are present in the file and invisible to every route and to the index.
   195	8. **Copies:**
   196	   - a backup taken while the writer commits passes an integrity check;
   197	   - preflight refuses a store whose writer holds the lock under any alias (the store's own path, a symlink and a hard link
   198	     elsewhere);
   199	   - preflight arriving just before the writer: whichever takes the lock first wins. A writer finding preflight's lock refuses to
   200	     start and writes nothing; preflight finding the writer's lock refuses to copy;
   201	   - it accepts a crashed store (lock released by the operating system), a store stopped in order with its WAL kept, a snapshot,
   202	     and every historical input it accepts today;
   203	   - a writer started while preflight holds the lock waits or refuses, and never writes under the copy.
   204	8a. **Lexical search under an unfinished day:** a paused writer and a crashed one, each with more than 50 unfinished views matching a
   205	   query. The search at the last completed day equals the same search on that day's backup: the same results, ranks and mode.
   206	8b. **The index:**
   207	   - a refresh that fails after one successful batch leaves the published day and the search results unchanged;
   208	   - a retry with the same target completes and advances the day;
   209	   - a retry after another day has completed reuses exactly the staged rows whose four key parts match;
   210	   - two stores at the same path with the same first sequence and day but different identities: no staged row of the first is
   211	     reused for the second, and no published vector of the first is used for the second;
   212	   - a store restored from an earlier snapshot, with the same identity but a published day after its last completed day: its
   213	     published vectors are not used until a refresh.
   214	8d. **Orderly stop:** with a service reader holding the store, the writer's stop leaves WAL mode in place, records it and releases the
   215	   lock; preflight then accepts the store. With no reader, the store ends in rollback-journal mode.
   216	8c. **The index:** on a store with an unfinished day, the views sent to the (fake) embedding service are exactly those visible as of
   217	   the last completed day. After the day completes, the index is extended to it, and search names the index's day until then.
   218	9. **The resolver keeps legacy and empty stores as today:** a legacy ledger with no `days_completed` and an empty store give the
   219	   same answers before and after `resolve_bound` is introduced.
   220	10. **The paused-writer harness** (`page-contract/serving_while_writing.py`) re-run: every check holds and the first-day case passes.
